Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:04.710 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  ahoy michael here to finally cover one  of the zaniest and most beloved animes  to ever sail the great blue that's right  one piece the story of luffy d monkey's  quest to become king of the pirates is  filled with unforgettable characters  incredible adventures and a literal  boatload of laughs  but is there something deeper happening  beneath the chaotic waves specifically  can one piece actually tell us something  about organized society or even how to  rebel against a society that sucks we  think so and we'll explain in this  wisecrack edition on one piece how to  live outside the law and spoilers ahead  for about a fifth of the total run of  one piece but if you like this let us  know and we can make another video in  the future for the uninitiated one piece  is a show about luffy an aspiring pirate  king who rounds up a crew of misfits to  sail the sea in pursuit of the titular  one-piece treasure these aren't your  run-of-the-mill kooks either luffy is a  rubber man meaning he can stretch his  body mr fantastic style a power he  indulges to adorable effect at every  opportunity  his pal zorro is a pirate hunter turned  pirate who wields not one not two but  three swords one is for his mouth  obviously nami reads relatively normal  but she's still a sneaky thief and  kick-ass navigator the chef sanji is a  horned up nicotine fiend who loves fine  dining kicking ass their engineer yusuf  is a pathological liar who has a tough  time distinguishing his fantasies from  reality oh and their doctor is a magical  deer who can talk when we say misfits we  mean it  [Music]  that said maybe a magical deer doctor  wouldn't be on my case about my  cholesterol levels so much and these  aren't the only weirdos in this world in  fact almost every single character is  bizarre and that's largely the point the  farther from the mainland you go the  stranger the adventures become it's  almost like when you're out at sea the  rules of land or the logic of terrain no  longer apply so why does this matter  we can answer with the help of bald icon  leather aficionado and french  philosopher michel foucault let's start  with what foucault thought about terrain  and rhodes in his essay of other spaces  foucault explained that different spaces  in modern society function as a network  that is they exist in relation to one  another he writes that one could  describe via the cluster of relations  that allows them to be defined the sites  of temporary relaxation cafes cinemas  beaches likewise one could describe via  its network of relations the closed or  semi-closed sites of rest the house the  bedroom the bed etc this is to say that  there are places where certain rules  apply you don't run around screaming in  a movie theater and you aren't getting  into bed to do a jazzercise routine  these places have defined functions and  as such have defined rules we see plenty  of the expectations surrounding various  kinds of land-based spaces in one piece  like when luffy is caught breaking the  law  which is sort of his favorite hobby or  when the crew is forced to confront the  reality of farming among other  landlocked jobs and the costs that come  with them however foucault points out  that some spaces are free from these  sorts of rules these he calls  heterotopia and unlike what you might be  thinking heterotopias don't refer to a  buffalo wild wings next to a gap outlet  heterotopias are typically enclosed  spaces  worlds within worlds if you will  fittingly for our purposes he identifies  the boat as a heterotopia par excellence  describing the boat as a floating piece  of space a place without a place that  exists by itself that is closed in on  itself and at the same time is given  over to the infinity of the sea when it  comes to modern transportation the way  we move has become largely codified we  drive cars on government paved roads at  government-enforced speed limits and  take our shoes off for the privilege of  flying across the country in a rattling  tin can with wings but when you traverse  the sea you're a lot more free and  unrestricted sure there are laws but as  anyone who saw captain phillips knows  it's a lot easier to break them a  heterotopia's specific function and  population creates a unique type of  freedom for its inhabitants as scholar  ibrahim marazka explains a heterotopia  enables human agency because it allows  the freedom to create other  rationalities and to follow them think  of how on the going mary the crew can  act like absolute maniacs  [Music]  [Applause]  or how they can derail their quest at  any time to say help reinstall a missing  monarch or bring a dragon colony back to  life as luffy explains he doesn't want  to be pirate king because he wants  people to bow down to him he simply  wants to be free  and life on the going mary enables that  pure state of freedom heck luffy is  completely clueless about and ambivalent  towards money  and his life like his corporeal form is  pretty malleable throughout the cruise  adventures they play a wide variety of  roles and are able to go wherever they  choose sometimes they're saviors  sometimes they're criminals sometimes  they're just trying to enjoy a dinner  uninterrupted by pirate fights and it's  not just that they move around a lot and  make zany friends they also use this  freedom to fight against oppressive  powers that might seek to restrict them  the crew often finds itself in places  run by corrupt governments or taken over  by the marines or even usurped by evil  pirates who not coincidentally have  often given up their seafaring gig for a  life on land fittingly foucault wrote  that in civilizations without boats  dreams dry up espionage replaces  adventure and the police the pirates we  see this literalized repeatedly in one  piece which takes us to places where  pirates have given up the freedom of  heterotopias to impose a very not fun  rule of law obviously this isn't the  only story where a crew of pirates  represent radical freedom but the cool  thing about one piece is how explicit it  is we see a world government that is  corrupt and uses its power to oppress  people and inflict harm on weaker  communities we see our heroes frequently  matched up against these evil systems  and according to scholar julian reid the  freedom afforded to nomadic groups like  pirates puts them in the best possible  position to take on powerful enemies  wisecrack favorites gil de luz and felix  guattari drew an important distinction  between two visions of war  there's war as a state apparatus that  uses violence to control its people  intimidate enemies or be straight up  jerks and then there's the war machine  i.e the eternal human impulse to  struggle sometimes violently for the  sake of differentiation that is a  struggle in which people want to affirm  themselves on their own terms rather  than in relation to some opposing force  it's basically the opposite of what  philosophers call dialectical thought  which always defines two opposites  precisely in their opposition to one  another i.e  hot and cold up and down taco bell and  pepto-bismol as reid explains de la's  thought that when we define war as an  instrument of only the state apparatus  we create limitations because war exists  both inside and outside the domain of  political sovereignty  basically dallas thinks you don't need  to be a nation to wage a war in fact he  uses evidence from ethnography to argue  that nomadic tribes often waged war not  to protect a state but to stop a  centralized state from forming in the  first place  in this view war has been and still can  be a valuable form of resistance against  state control and we recently did a  video arguing that the human impulse for  war isn't really that natural so be sure  and check that out for a different  perspective because of this deluxe sees  the war machine as inherently  antagonistic to the state apparatus  though the state may try its darndest to  appropriate it deluz ultimately believed  that the war machine could be  transformative and offer alternatives to  the state which he thought limited  possibilities  take the opposing ways that states and  nomads navigate space  states organize it determine ownership  of it and place other arbitrary  restrictions on it which is why i'm no  longer welcome at yosemite national park  it's a long story  in contrast nomads live freely without  territorializing the spaces they inhabit  states might define nomads in relation  to themselves but nomads driving the war  machine are striving for a pure  affirmation that differentiates them  from the state we see the nomadic war  machine throughout one piece pretty much  every narrative arc is resolved with a  big fight with the stakes almost always  being the freedom of a character or an  entire community or sometimes just a  cute dragon like the time luffy encode  take on arlong or fight to liberate  alabasta and this is a key way in which  the anarchic nature of the going mary's  crew can teach us about the world they  are violent but their violence maintains  a code and it's nomatic and that it's  often directed against the state they  don't belong to a military structure  that serves the needs and demands of the  sovereign what's more remember how our  friends live in a heterotopia that gives  them the ability to see outside the  rules and norms of organized society as  a result they aren't confined to a  single world view you could call them  psychic nomads that doesn't mean they're  wanderers with telekinesis psychic  nomadism simply means that you pick and  choose from a great big buffet of world  views religious political ethical while  rejecting aspects of those views that  you don't find as tasty for example  luffy embraces the violence and chaos of  the pirate worldview but rejects the  cruelty and selfishness and chopper  views the world through the lens of  magic being a talking therinol while  still abiding by the science of medicine  the result can be as you might guess  somewhat chaotic sometimes the crew has  to save a kingdom and install a rightful  ruler sometimes they have to get rid of  rulers altogether it all depends on the  scenario and on who happens to be on  board the ship rather than fighting one  political ideology with another they're  fighting against ideology altogether and  defending some type of formless freedom  or self-actualization they don't want to  exist in relation to the state they just  want to exist  the key consistency though is that the  crew resists the monopoly over violence  granted to those in power in large part  thanks to their relative egalitarianism  now of course we're not saying that you  should buy a boat and sail around the  florida panhandle to beat up the coast  guard  but if that's your bag who are we to  stop you but you can still take a  valuable lesson from one piece modern  social movements are often under a lot  of pressure to strive for ideological  consistency when they resist state power  in contrast the one-piece crew doesn't  stake a consistent position in their  resistance the way they fight back  doesn't always have to look the same or  uphold the same values rather they use  their flexibility and their position as  residents of a heterotopia to change  themselves to fit the crisis at hand  this mutability as a form of resistance  is very literally displayed in luffy who  can stretch his body in all sorts of  forms as he fights the crew's mobility  both literally and ideologically is its  own form of power and maybe more  importantly for our swashbuckling homies  it's a form of positive freedom that is  a freedom which is more about freedom  for  rather than a freedom from some type of  oppression or control in this way one  piece shows us the value of movement and  flexibility and the unique power of the  pirate and the nomad these lifestyles  that are able to adjust change and  accommodate new circumstances aren't  just excuses to wear cool hats and bling  but are in fact ways to more effectively  think about ourselves as political and  social actors but what do you guys think  does the crew show us the power of the  nomadic war machine should we ditch land  for the pure heterotopic freedom of a  boat let us know in the comments big  thanks to our patrons for all your  support and be sure to check out our  revamped patreon page for loads of  exclusive new content stab that  subscribe button like you're cutting a  steak with your mouth knife and don't  forget to ring that bell and as always  thanks for watching later  [Music]  energy okay sorry that hurt your ears if  you had headphones on

